The best approach is to use the **`System.IO.Abstractions`** NuGet package rather than writing a custom wrapper — it's battle-tested and covers all three calls you mentioned.

### 1. Install the packages

```bash
dotnet add package System.IO.Abstractions
dotnet add <YourTestProject> package System.IO.Abstractions.TestingHelpers
```

### 2. Register in DI

```csharp
// In Program.cs / Startup.cs
builder.Services.AddSingleton<IFileSystem, FileSystem>();
```

### 3. Inject `IFileSystem` into your classes

```csharp
public class ConfigManager(IFileSystem fileSystem)
{
    public string Read(string path)
        => fileSystem.File.ReadAllText(path);

    public void Write(string path, string content)
        => fileSystem.File.WriteAllText(path, content);

    public bool EnsureDirectory(string path)
        => fileSystem.Directory.Exists(path);
}
```

### 4. Test with `MockFileSystem`

```csharp
var mockFs = new MockFileSystem(new Dictionary<string, MockFileData>
{
    { "/data/config.json", new MockFileData("{\"key\": \"value\"}") }
});

var manager = new ConfigManager(mockFs);

Assert.Equal("{\"key\": \"value\"}", manager.Read("/data/config.json"));

manager.Write("/data/out.txt", "hello");
Assert.Equal("hello", mockFs.File.ReadAllText("/data/out.txt"));

Assert.True(manager.EnsureDirectory("/data"));
Assert.False(manager.EnsureDirectory("/nope"));
```

**Key points:**
- `IFileSystem.File` mirrors `System.IO.File` and `IFileSystem.Directory` mirrors `System.IO.Directory` — so call-site changes are minimal.
- Register as **singleton** (it's stateless).
- `MockFileSystem` gives you a full in-memory filesystem for tests — no temp files needed.

